WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.902
This is a really nice room!

2
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.425
Yeah, it sounds thick.
Too bad he's not coming.

3
00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:15.095
Look, I talked to him myself.
He'll be here.

4
00:00:15.096 --> 00:00:15.747
Right.

5
00:00:15.747 --> 00:00:19.220
You're the one who said the song could use a horn part, so I got the best.

6
00:00:19.263 --> 00:00:19.928
John Henry!

7
00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.096
You think I'm stupid?

8
00:00:21.096 --> 00:00:23.345
Of course I do, but that's not the point.

9
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.203
He'll be here, okay?

10
00:00:33.745 --> 00:00:34.911
Take a left up ahead.

11
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.409
South Street? I cancelled this gig.

12
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:41.221
You said you didn't want to do any sessions for a while.

13
00:00:41.221 --> 00:00:42.345
For a while.

14
00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.383
I called her, I un-cancelled it.

15
00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.182
But you up to it?

16
00:00:46.182 --> 00:00:47.566
Let's find out.

17
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.367
You lied to me, John Henry's never gonna show up!

18
00:00:56.262 --> 00:00:57.355
Will you stop?

19
00:00:57.356 --> 00:00:59.010
It's been, like, two hours.

20
00:00:59.011 --> 00:01:00.320
And we will keep waiting.

21
00:01:00.321 --> 00:01:04.365
I mean, you've heard this guy play
and you know what he does,
so can you just shut up, Tommy?

22
00:01:07.227 --> 00:01:08.381
Oh, my God!

23
00:01:09.794 --> 00:01:10.645
Am I late?

24
00:01:20.449 --> 00:01:21.683
You need to hear it again?

25
00:01:22.788 --> 00:01:24.658
Nope, I get it. Let's try one.

26
00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:26.773
See what we get.
Let's do it.

27
00:02:00.563 --> 00:02:04.042
John! You okay? You okay?

28
00:02:04.108 --> 00:02:05.788
I can't breathe, I can't breathe.

29
00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:07.110
[coughing]

30
00:02:07.153 --> 00:02:08.680
Call an ambulance!

31
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.194
Now!

32
00:02:15.945 --> 00:02:20.496
1X09: DNR

33
00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.158
Original Airdate on FOX : February 1, 2005

34
00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.100
I want in.

35
00:03:01.562 --> 00:03:03.637
John Henry Giles, you a fan of his music?

36
00:03:03.637 --> 00:03:04.550
He's a musician?

37
00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:11.583
That paralysis thing, guy can't walk for two years and nobody knows why, that seems mildly interesting.

38
00:03:11.584 --> 00:03:13.313
Forget his paralysis.

39
00:03:13.313 --> 00:03:14.931
Tell that to the rest of his bowling team.

40
00:03:15.347 --> 00:03:18.882
As far as this hospital is concerned,
this is a simple case of low-bar pneumonia.

41
00:03:18.883 --> 00:03:19.577
Boring.

42
00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:22.276
But that "not walking" thing, that could turn into something serious!

43
00:03:22.858 --> 00:03:26.415
Marty Hamilton is his primary physician out in California.

44
00:03:26.457 --> 00:03:27.621
He's dealing with the paralysis.

45
00:03:27.664 --> 00:03:28.395
Know all about it.

46
00:03:28.396 --> 00:03:30.488
Multiple treatments,
multiple surgeries.

47
00:03:30.512 --> 00:03:33.208
Making real progress.
Fixed everything but the legs.

48
00:03:33.251 --> 00:03:35.813
Dr. Hamilton already called and asked for your team.

49
00:03:36.063 --> 00:03:39.043
And by team, I don't mean you.

50
00:03:39.444 --> 00:03:41.563
Like I always say, there's no "I" in "team".

51
00:03:42.297 --> 00:03:44.233
There is a "me", though, if you jumble it up.

52
00:03:45.433 --> 00:03:47.325
Foreman did his residency with Hamilton.

53
00:03:47.325 --> 00:03:51.011
I know. I did accidentally glance at his resume before I hired him.

54
00:03:51.012 --> 00:03:52.670
He wants someone he can trust.

55
00:03:52.713 --> 00:03:54.567
He must have spoken to Foreman's parole officer.

56
00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.351
Someone who will stick to the pneumonia.

57
00:03:57.393 --> 00:04:00.558
John Henry's on an experimental protocol for the paralysis.

58
00:04:00.601 --> 00:04:02.419
I respect that.
I'm not going to get in his way.

59
00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:04.015
It's Foreman's case.

60
00:04:04.765 --> 00:04:06.497
It's pneumonia, he can handle it.

61
00:04:07.015 --> 00:04:09.762
Guy's already paralyzed,
how badly can he screw it up?

62
00:04:26.421 --> 00:04:28.456
So, what are his stats?

63
00:04:28.498 --> 00:04:30.462
Staying in the 90s on the nasal canula.

64
00:04:30.505 --> 00:04:31.438
Coughing up much sputa?

65
00:04:31.481 --> 00:04:33.370
Almost none, he seems to be stabilized.

66
00:04:33.413 --> 00:04:34.953
Dr. House, is there anything back from Micro?

67
00:04:34.995 --> 00:04:37.437
Not yet. You gonna fire me?

68
00:04:37.438 --> 00:04:39.186
You can make up for it by washing my car.

69
00:04:39.556 --> 00:04:40.906
Oh, this is fun.

70
00:04:41.074 --> 00:04:43.432
Let's keep him on the broad-spectrum antibiotics,

71
00:04:43.475 --> 00:04:45.667
and since he's displaying septic physiology,

72
00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:47.680
draw blood for adrenal and thyroid function.

73
00:04:50.588 --> 00:04:52.039
How about the paralysis?

74
00:04:52.208 --> 00:04:53.527
We're sticking to the pneumonia.

75
00:04:53.612 --> 00:04:55.189
Well, you certainly are, boss.

76
00:04:55.232 --> 00:04:57.455
Like a wet tongue sticks to dry ice.

77
00:04:57.497 --> 00:05:00.115
The paralysis has already been diagnosed by Dr. Hamilton.

78
00:05:00.201 --> 00:05:01.029
It's ALS.

79
00:05:01.072 --> 00:05:03.804
Lou Gehrig's disease.
It's a lovely diagnosis.

80
00:05:03.846 --> 00:05:04.969
They make movies about it.

81
00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.293
No tests, no treatment.
It's a disease of exclusion.

82
00:05:08.378 --> 00:05:10.385
because Hamilton has excluded everything else.

83
00:05:10.428 --> 00:05:11.242
I haven't.

84
00:05:16.317 --> 00:05:17.321
What else could it be?

85
00:05:18.243 --> 00:05:20.362
Guillain-Barre, which would be reversible.

86
00:05:20.404 --> 00:05:21.101
Excellent.

87
00:05:21.164 --> 00:05:24.600
No, the progression of the paralysis would be symmetric.

88
00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:25.408
This wasn't.

89
00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:26.483
Transverse myelitis.

90
00:05:26.526 --> 00:05:27.842
Hamilton tested for it.
Negative.

91
00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:29.865
And he was negative for masses, and AVM, and...

92
00:05:29.908 --> 00:05:31.568
Antibodies could be attacking the nerve.

93
00:05:31.569 --> 00:05:33.018
Multifocal motoneuropathy.

94
00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.787
Uncommon, but it fits.
It's also treatable.

95
00:05:36.028 --> 00:05:38.138
Did Hamilton try putting the guy on IVIG?

96
00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.327
No, because the MRI showed...

97
00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:41.037
Well, let's do an MRI of our own.

98
00:05:45.465 --> 00:05:47.708
Guys? It's my case.

99
00:05:49.151 --> 00:05:51.543
ALS fits. It even predicts the pneumonia.

100
00:05:51.815 --> 00:05:53.317
The paralysis is progressive.

101
00:05:53.999 --> 00:05:55.303
It's a death sentence.

102
00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.777
That doesn't make it wrong.

103
00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:19.297
So, you think the breathing stuff is connected to my ALS?

104
00:06:19.961 --> 00:06:20.957
It makes sense.

105
00:06:23.719 --> 00:06:25.399
So, it's just gonna get worse, huh?

106
00:06:25.399 --> 00:06:26.812
Well, Dr. Hamilton...

107
00:06:26.896 --> 00:06:31.284
Great guy. Really smart doctor,
but his treatment obviously isn't working.

108
00:06:33.572 --> 00:06:36.495
Do you think I'm gonna die here, or
you think I'm gonna get to go back home first?

109
00:06:37.349 --> 00:06:39.204
An MRI would give us a better idea...

110
00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:41.602
An MRI? Come on.
For pneumonia?

111
00:06:41.645 --> 00:06:42.964
Well, Dr. House thinks we should test for...

112
00:06:43.007 --> 00:06:47.288
House? Yeah, I heard about him.
Obsessive son-of-a-bitch?

113
00:06:47.289 --> 00:06:48.514
[chuckling] That's him.

114
00:06:50.766 --> 00:06:52.802
So, who do you think is right?
Hamilton or House?

115
00:06:53.917 --> 00:06:56.324
They're both excellent physicians.

116
00:06:56.378 --> 00:06:57.366
Come on.

117
00:06:58.119 --> 00:07:01.784
One of 'em says ALS, the other one says not ALS.

118
00:07:02.413 --> 00:07:03.986
You gotta pick one, son.

119
00:07:04.029 --> 00:07:07.475
[sighs] Everything points to ALS.

120
00:07:08.797 --> 00:07:09.937
Then no MRI.

121
00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:19.080
And I want one of them papers that say I, I don't want nothin' done if something go bad.

122
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.357
A DNR?

123
00:07:21.527 --> 00:07:23.239
Mr. Giles, you don't want to rush things...

124
00:07:23.281 --> 00:07:25.002
It's been two years, I ain't rushing.

125
00:07:25.502 --> 00:07:26.766
I wanna sign one.

126
00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:33.658
Now, while my arm still works.

127
00:07:59.431 --> 00:08:00.727
He signed a DNR.

128
00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:05.829
He rhymes with dinner?

129
00:08:06.952 --> 00:08:08.501
He signed a DNR.

130
00:08:09.037 --> 00:08:11.606
Oh. That makes more sense.

131
00:08:12.945 --> 00:08:14.571
You tell him it might not be ALS?

132
00:08:15.138 --> 00:08:15.798
No.

133
00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:19.232
Well, no wonder he signed.
Who wouldn't?

134
00:08:19.467 --> 00:08:22.136
I started him on IV steroids and ancinthroid.

135
00:08:22.179 --> 00:08:22.720
Great.

136
00:08:23.702 --> 00:08:26.979
If it was my case, I'd be adding a little IVIG to the mix.

137
00:08:27.106 --> 00:08:28.268
For his pneumonia?

138
00:08:28.684 --> 00:08:30.536
That's my story and I'm sticking to it.

139
00:08:32.001 --> 00:08:34.474
He doesn't want anything done.
No treatment.

140
00:08:35.188 --> 00:08:38.803
DNR means "do not resuscitate", not "do not treat".

141
00:08:40.018 --> 00:08:42.508
You do nothing, it doesn't matter which one of us is right.

142
00:08:45.724 --> 00:08:47.734
And hang on to that DNR.

143
00:08:47.837 --> 00:08:50.353
That signature could be worth a lot of money real soon.

144
00:08:57.789 --> 00:08:59.840
This is Dr. Foreman.
What's Mr. Giles's status?

145
00:09:01.187 --> 00:09:04.064
Okay, increase the steroids to 100mg every 12 hours,

146
00:09:04.065 --> 00:09:07.217
and, ah, start him on IVIG.

147
00:09:07.792 --> 00:09:10.525
Yeah, I'm sure. One gram per kilo.

148
00:09:12.557 --> 00:09:14.213
My nature isn't what it used to be.

149
00:09:14.556 --> 00:09:17.177
The little man has lost some bounce in his step.

150
00:09:17.389 --> 00:09:20.242
He needs to crank it up,
have himself some fun this weekend.

151
00:09:21.331 --> 00:09:22.868
He wants the blue pills.

152
00:09:23.443 --> 00:09:25.872
You're talking about your penis in the third person.

153
00:09:25.956 --> 00:09:28.097
Me and him, two people.

154
00:09:28.233 --> 00:09:29.315
Separate vacations?

155
00:09:29.789 --> 00:09:31.377
That'd be a drag for one of you.

156
00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:32.989
I don't think you need the pills.

157
00:09:33.221 --> 00:09:35.244
I think you have a conflict of medications.

158
00:09:35.689 --> 00:09:38.797
You need to up your insulin to "chocolate chip ice cream" levels.

159
00:09:39.407 --> 00:09:40.092
Insulin?

160
00:09:40.251 --> 00:09:41.261
Yeah, you remember.

161
00:09:41.524 --> 00:09:44.714
That's the stuff you take for the diabetes that you forgot to tell the nurse about.

162
00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:46.860
Your hands.

163
00:09:47.673 --> 00:09:50.828
No hair, which means nerve damage.

164
00:09:50.932 --> 00:09:55.664
And your shoes look about two sizes too small which means you've lost sensation in your feet.

165
00:09:56.084 --> 00:09:57.642
And then there's your pants.

166
00:09:57.989 --> 00:09:59.674
My pants tell you I have diabetes?

167
00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:01.318
No, they tell me you're an idiot.

168
00:10:01.804 --> 00:10:03.741
Powdered sugar on the right pant leg.

169
00:10:05.031 --> 00:10:06.994
Based on the two napkins in your right pocket,

170
00:10:07.121 --> 00:10:09.598
I'm willing to bet it's not your first donut of the day.

171
00:10:18.295 --> 00:10:19.555
You're giving me the pills?

172
00:10:20.058 --> 00:10:21.185
Sure, why not?

173
00:10:21.915 --> 00:10:24.599
If you've got heart disease from ignoring the diabetes they'll kill you.

174
00:10:26.224 --> 00:10:28.835
Otherwise, you two have a fun weekend.

175
00:10:38.195 --> 00:10:39.427
0-2 stats are dropping.

176
00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:40.309
Breath sounds?

177
00:10:40.352 --> 00:10:40.856
Junky.

178
00:10:40.898 --> 00:10:41.554
Mucus plug?

179
00:10:41.597 --> 00:10:44.235
No, it's our mistake.
He's sludging from the IVIG.

180
00:10:44.331 --> 00:10:45.497
Blood can't get through to his lungs.

181
00:10:45.539 --> 00:10:47.208
Start Heparin. 500ccs, IV push.

182
00:10:47.251 --> 00:10:50.400
He won't last long enough for it to work.
We've got to intubate him, stat.

183
00:10:51.245 --> 00:10:51.812
We can't!

184
00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:53.498
He's DNR.

185
00:10:53.583 --> 00:10:54.285
What?!

186
00:11:03.234 --> 00:11:04.423
Someone likes music.

187
00:11:04.802 --> 00:11:05.607
Oh, yeah.

188
00:11:12.779 --> 00:11:14.596
His stats are in the 70s.
How long has he been like this?

189
00:11:14.639 --> 00:11:15.494
10 minutes.

190
00:11:15.494 --> 00:11:17.718
- Well, why aren't you doing ?
- The IVIG put him into respiratory failure.

191
00:11:17.761 --> 00:11:19.081
You put him on the IVIG?

192
00:11:19.166 --> 00:11:20.092
We did this.

193
00:11:20.454 --> 00:11:22.140
So undo it! Chase!

194
00:11:22.428 --> 00:11:23.861
It's too late! We killed him.

195
00:11:23.861 --> 00:11:25.595
Nobody killed him! He's not dead!

196
00:11:25.723 --> 00:11:26.841
Chase, intubate him!

197
00:11:27.461 --> 00:11:28.622
He's DNR.

198
00:11:39.729 --> 00:11:40.979
What are you doing?

199
00:11:46.559 --> 00:11:47.937
You can't do this!

200
00:11:50.237 --> 00:11:51.085
Bag.

201
00:11:51.244 --> 00:11:52.659
You can't do this!

202
00:11:52.743 --> 00:11:53.632
Bag!

203
00:12:02.319 --> 00:12:04.590
He had a bad reaction to some medicine we gave him.

204
00:12:05.986 --> 00:12:07.266
What did you just do?

205
00:12:09.065 --> 00:12:10.371
He saved his life.

206
00:12:23.903 --> 00:12:26.666
He's stable on the ventilator.
Oxygenating well.

207
00:12:26.708 --> 00:12:28.456
The IVIG made him worse,

208
00:12:28.626 --> 00:12:32.043
which means multifocal motoneuropathy was a bad diagnosis.

209
00:12:32.311 --> 00:12:33.662
Okay, what's really wrong with him?

210
00:12:33.704 --> 00:12:35.174
What's wrong with you?

211
00:12:35.174 --> 00:12:38.109
Everyone knows what's wrong with me.
What's wrong with him is much more interesting.

212
00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:40.198
You tubed him and he didn't wanna be tubed!

213
00:12:40.414 --> 00:12:42.389
He has a legal paper saying just that.

214
00:12:42.414 --> 00:12:45.610
To intubate or not to intubate, that is the big ethical question.

215
00:12:45.864 --> 00:12:48.493
Actually, I was hoping we could avoid it,
maybe just practice some medicine.

216
00:12:48.494 --> 00:12:49.853
There's no question.

217
00:12:50.033 --> 00:12:51.527
It's the patient's decision...

218
00:12:51.569 --> 00:12:55.847
...if the patient is competent to make it,
if his thyroid numbers aren't making him sad.

219
00:12:55.848 --> 00:12:57.277
Oh, my God.
You don't believe that.

220
00:12:57.319 --> 00:12:58.560
His thyroid levels were a little...

221
00:12:58.603 --> 00:13:01.214
It's nothing.
Do not defend him.

222
00:13:01.235 --> 00:13:02.830
Why did he sign that DNR?

223
00:13:03.149 --> 00:13:04.107
I didn't talk him into...

224
00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:08.290
No, he signed the DNR 'cause he didn't want a slow and painful death from ALS.

225
00:13:08.333 --> 00:13:10.263
What was happening to him had nothing to do with his ALS.

226
00:13:10.306 --> 00:13:13.208
Right! Exactly! It's the IVIG, you screwed up!

227
00:13:13.723 --> 00:13:16.117
You're not gonna let him die because you screwed up!

228
00:13:17.365 --> 00:13:20.261
Technically, your case.
You screwed up.

229
00:13:20.851 --> 00:13:21.866
Is that what this is about?

230
00:13:21.927 --> 00:13:23.779
Looking bad in front of your old boss?

231
00:13:23.815 --> 00:13:25.050
You assaulted that man.

232
00:13:25.051 --> 00:13:26.630
Fine. I'll never do it again.

233
00:13:26.631 --> 00:13:27.437
Yes, you will.

234
00:13:27.437 --> 00:13:29.320
All the more reason this debate is pointless.

235
00:13:35.237 --> 00:13:38.243
His lungs are worse. Any theories?

236
00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.753
Oh, I'm sure he just went to the little boys' room.

237
00:13:41.839 --> 00:13:42.741
Come on, people.

238
00:13:42.742 --> 00:13:44.708
Uh, vasculitis?

239
00:13:44.751 --> 00:13:46.142
Wouldn't likely hit both lungs.

240
00:13:46.184 --> 00:13:48.275
It could be Wegener's granulomatosis.

241
00:13:49.077 --> 00:13:49.999
Dr. House?

242
00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:54.946
Cuddy sent me a stripper again?

243
00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:57.440
Ah, love that woman.
So thoughtful.

244
00:13:58.346 --> 00:13:59.119
Sorry.

245
00:14:00.632 --> 00:14:03.522
There are case reports of Wegener's hitting both the legs and the spine.

246
00:14:03.522 --> 00:14:05.508
Well, it's not great, but it's better than ALS.

247
00:14:05.599 --> 00:14:06.874
At least it's treatable.

248
00:14:06.917 --> 00:14:07.889
It's a restraining order.

249
00:14:08.059 --> 00:14:10.497
You're not to come within 50 feet of John Henry Giles,

250
00:14:10.497 --> 00:14:13.693
and they've asked the DA to file criminal charges for battery.

251
00:14:16.363 --> 00:14:18.445
Cameron, test the blood for C-anke (sp).

252
00:14:19.269 --> 00:14:20.570
These are criminal charges.

253
00:14:20.614 --> 00:14:22.726
They aren't going to let you take blood to make more tests.

254
00:14:22.769 --> 00:14:25.359
He has blood left in the lab, just add on the c-anke.

255
00:14:25.654 --> 00:14:28.404
Foreman's still got you doing bronchioscopic suctioning for the pneumonia?

256
00:14:28.447 --> 00:14:29.385
Every four hours.

257
00:14:29.428 --> 00:14:31.962
Well, while you're down in his lungs, grab a biopsy.

258
00:14:31.963 --> 00:14:33.499
We'll need it to confirm Wegener's.

259
00:14:34.297 --> 00:14:36.341
And move the patient to the second floor ICU.

260
00:14:36.341 --> 00:14:36.812
Why?

261
00:14:36.813 --> 00:14:37.976
It's right about the clinic.

262
00:14:38.514 --> 00:14:40.653
I'm pretty sure it's 50 feet in any direction.

263
00:14:41.771 --> 00:14:44.701
It's nice having a court order saying you don't have to work clinic duty.

264
00:15:01.987 --> 00:15:03.066
Dr. Cuddy!

265
00:15:03.699 --> 00:15:04.543
Excuse me.

266
00:15:06.527 --> 00:15:07.331
You paged me.

267
00:15:07.331 --> 00:15:08.405
Why the yelling?

268
00:15:09.289 --> 00:15:10.918
His bed is behind that wall.

269
00:15:11.228 --> 00:15:12.438
The plaintiff's.

270
00:15:12.819 --> 00:15:15.247
I can't, you know, come any closer.

271
00:15:16.355 --> 00:15:18.494
It's right over the clinic. Very cute.

272
00:15:18.765 --> 00:15:21.159
Can we get the lecture over with, because I've got a...

273
00:15:22.902 --> 00:15:24.615
actually, I've got nothing to do.
Take your time.

274
00:15:25.866 --> 00:15:27.068
You need a lawyer?

275
00:15:28.184 --> 00:15:29.851
You don't have a problem with what I did?

276
00:15:30.658 --> 00:15:33.060
When I hired you,
I knew you were insane.

277
00:15:33.061 --> 00:15:37.733
I will continue to try and stop you from doing insane things, but once they're done...

278
00:15:38.776 --> 00:15:43.681
Trying to convince an insane person not to do insane things is, in itself, insane,

279
00:15:43.683 --> 00:15:47.961
so when I hired you I also set aside $50,000 a year for legal expenses.

280
00:15:49.356 --> 00:15:50.851
So far you've come in under budget.

281
00:15:51.957 --> 00:15:52.783
Great.

282
00:15:53.982 --> 00:15:55.794
Any chance you could help me run some tests?

283
00:15:56.818 --> 00:16:00.337
Dr. Foreman called Dr. Hamilton.
He's flying in from L.A.

284
00:16:01.355 --> 00:16:02.501
You can talk to him.

285
00:16:03.126 --> 00:16:09.178
Dr. Hamilton is flying in as a friend of John Henry's to pull the plug.

286
00:16:13.734 --> 00:16:16.354
Yeah. I need a lawyer.

287
00:16:21.398 --> 00:16:23.839
Your Honor, on behalf of Gregory House,

288
00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:30.697
we have convened an emergency session of this court to bring a motion requesting John Henry Giles remain on life support.

289
00:16:30.752 --> 00:16:37.474
Mr. House faces criminal charges for battery against...John Henry Giles.

290
00:16:38.461 --> 00:16:40.237
You beat up a guy in a wheelchair?

291
00:16:40.237 --> 00:16:46.004
Dr. House is alleged to have forced a tube down Mr. Giles' throat against his will.

292
00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:49.569
A medical tube, saving his life.

293
00:16:49.924 --> 00:16:52.870
Dr. House, please let your attorney speak for you.

294
00:16:55.265 --> 00:16:57.623
I'm sorry, your Honor.
I was way out of line.

295
00:16:59.876 --> 00:17:04.165
So, uh, your client forced the patient into this position,

296
00:17:04.166 --> 00:17:09.362
and now he wants a court order to force the patient to stay that way.

297
00:17:09.707 --> 00:17:13.286
Without the tube, there's a high likelihood that Mr. Giles will die.

298
00:17:13.286 --> 00:17:16.523
Well, I assume the patient knows that, he had a DNR.

299
00:17:16.525 --> 00:17:19.558
That's why your client is facing criminal charges, right?

300
00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:20.517
Exactly.

301
00:17:20.561 --> 00:17:25.305
And Mr. Giles' death will violate my client's Sixth Amendment right.

302
00:17:25.579 --> 00:17:27.745
His right to face his accuser.

303
00:17:29.066 --> 00:17:30.366
That's clever, huh?

304
00:17:30.414 --> 00:17:34.036
Your Honor, in Commonwealth of Pennsylvania v. Hozelton,

305
00:17:34.079 --> 00:17:39.563
the 3rd district ruled that a defendant may not use that status to rule against a felony charge.

306
00:17:39.648 --> 00:17:42.147
Why are you doing this?
It's not going to keep you out of jail.

307
00:17:42.189 --> 00:17:42.732
No.

308
00:17:42.733 --> 00:17:47.170
Even if you win, the restraining order and battery charge stay in place.

309
00:17:47.171 --> 00:17:48.242
What have you gained?

310
00:17:48.242 --> 00:17:49.114
Time.

311
00:17:49.713 --> 00:17:52.074
To diagnose him?
You can't get near him!

312
00:17:52.098 --> 00:17:53.842
I don't want to get near him.

313
00:17:54.197 --> 00:17:58.413
You know how some doctors have the Messiah complex, they need to save the world?

314
00:17:58.588 --> 00:18:02.059
You've got the Rubik's complex,
you need to solve the puzzle.

315
00:18:02.102 --> 00:18:05.889
Are you done, or do you have more references to 1980s fads?

316
00:18:06.058 --> 00:18:07.287
I'm trying to listen to this.

317
00:18:08.627 --> 00:18:10.780
That DNR may not be valid.

318
00:18:10.823 --> 00:18:14.711
The patient was suffering from depression brought on from a thyroid imbalance.

319
00:18:14.754 --> 00:18:21.482
The DNR order was witnessed by Dr. House's own staff, a, a Dr. Foreman.

320
00:18:21.524 --> 00:18:22.920
My staff are idiots.

321
00:18:23.414 --> 00:18:24.981
I'm sure you know what it's like, your Honor.

322
00:18:25.024 --> 00:18:26.376
Sit down!

323
00:18:30.518 --> 00:18:34.174
The validity of the DNR is a question of fact.

324
00:18:34.302 --> 00:18:38.050
Dr. House should have the opportunity to make his argument at a full trial.

325
00:18:38.093 --> 00:18:42.919
And this poor guy has gotta stay on life support until we can schedule a trial.

326
00:18:42.961 --> 00:18:43.700
Your Honor...

327
00:18:43.953 --> 00:18:49.387
Dr. House, I will hold you in contempt if you don't sit down.

328
00:18:49.478 --> 00:18:50.588
I have a medical issue.

329
00:18:50.588 --> 00:18:52.407
If it pertains to this case your attorney should...

330
00:18:52.449 --> 00:18:53.110
It doesn't.

331
00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:57.770
Do you have any history of heart disease in your family?

332
00:18:58.311 --> 00:18:59.150
Your Honor!

333
00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:03.262
Your fingers. They show signs of clubbing,
which indicate a health problem.

334
00:19:04.133 --> 00:19:05.525
Remember Bart Giamotti?

335
00:19:06.016 --> 00:19:07.954
Same thing, just dropped dead one day.

336
00:19:08.347 --> 00:19:09.586
Please see your doctor.

337
00:19:09.586 --> 00:19:13.986
He's admitted this isn't relevant.
Can we please get back to the motion?

338
00:19:14.836 --> 00:19:16.424
Of course, I'm sorry.

339
00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:22.666
Your Honor, a person's right to control the treatment of their own body

340
00:19:22.667 --> 00:19:26.924
is fundamental to understanding this case. A long line of ...

341
00:19:29.941 --> 00:19:33.244
Congratulations, impressive legal argument.

342
00:19:33.329 --> 00:19:34.862
I watched Matlock last night.

343
00:19:34.905 --> 00:19:37.927
Oh, say no more. I didn't notice any clubbing on the judge's fingers.

344
00:19:37.928 --> 00:19:38.705
Neither did I.

345
00:19:39.361 --> 00:19:41.143
So, the family history thing...

346
00:19:41.143 --> 00:19:43.915
Every family has some history of heart disease.

347
00:19:43.916 --> 00:19:45.580
And mental illness.

348
00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:51.542
Biopsy only shows inflammation.

349
00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:53.728
Start him on Cytoxan.

350
00:19:54.024 --> 00:19:56.372
You're diagnosis Wegener's based on a little inflammation?

351
00:19:56.499 --> 00:20:02.473
Well, unfortunately I think we've exhausted all the tests we can do in the capacity as the doctors he doesn't want doing tests on him.

352
00:20:02.474 --> 00:20:03.117
But you don't ...

353
00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:04.666
We'll know I'm right if he starts walking.

354
00:20:04.666 --> 00:20:07.020
You can't do any more tests,
but you want me to treat him?

355
00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:10.549
The medicine's in the pharmacy,
patient's immobilized in the ICU.

356
00:20:10.593 --> 00:20:12.545
Sounds like a simple geography problem.

357
00:20:14.221 --> 00:20:17.194
Are you asking us to jeopardize our medical licenses for this treatment?

358
00:20:43.801 --> 00:20:44.724
Can I help you?

359
00:20:45.991 --> 00:20:48.154
Uh, I'm Dr. House, I'm...

360
00:20:48.155 --> 00:20:49.079
Gregg, right?

361
00:20:49.123 --> 00:20:51.085
I'm Marty Hamilton,
John Henry's doctor.

362
00:20:53.766 --> 00:20:54.696
We should talk.

363
00:20:59.082 --> 00:21:01.619
I'd appreciate any list of medications, anything like that.

364
00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:03.301
I've started him on Cytoxan.

365
00:21:04.367 --> 00:21:05.795
For Wegener's, right?

366
00:21:06.467 --> 00:21:10.651
Look, Gregg.I checked you out, you know?

367
00:21:10.942 --> 00:21:12.186
I know you're a good doctor.

368
00:21:12.563 --> 00:21:14.372
You have to appreciate that I'm a good doctor, too.

369
00:21:15.027 --> 00:21:15.679
Why?

370
00:21:16.137 --> 00:21:18.057
Wegener's is one of the first things I looked for.

371
00:21:18.245 --> 00:21:20.895
The biopsy and the blood tests were negative, just like yours.

372
00:21:20.938 --> 00:21:22.914
There's an error rate, Marty.

373
00:21:23.318 --> 00:21:24.795
And there's a time to let go.

374
00:21:26.219 --> 00:21:29.504
Look, I'm gonna take him off the ventilator, and John Henry's gonna die.

375
00:21:29.506 --> 00:21:30.659
He's accepted that.

376
00:21:31.027 --> 00:21:32.385
Dr. Hamilton.

377
00:21:33.339 --> 00:21:35.917
Hey! How you doing?

378
00:21:36.595 --> 00:21:38.285
Listen, Dr. Hamilton, I'm sorry.

379
00:21:38.285 --> 00:21:39.969
I should have never put your patient on IVIG.

380
00:21:39.969 --> 00:21:40.629
It's not your fault, Eric.

381
00:21:40.672 --> 00:21:43.843
No, it's mine. Eric.

382
00:21:43.844 --> 00:21:45.111
That's not what I said.

383
00:21:45.463 --> 00:21:47.040
Everyone asks about you back in L.A.

384
00:21:47.261 --> 00:21:48.153
How's the old place doing?

385
00:21:48.153 --> 00:21:50.206
Oh, this is wonderful.

386
00:21:50.206 --> 00:21:52.330
But before you guys break out the oil,

387
00:21:52.355 --> 00:21:55.158
I should point out you can't pull the plug;
I have a court order.

388
00:21:56.974 --> 00:21:58.429
You used to, but...

389
00:21:58.471 --> 00:22:01.137
I have the right to face my accuser.
Judge said so.

390
00:22:01.138 --> 00:22:04.407
Not if no one's accusing you.
All the charges have been dropped.

391
00:22:12.116 --> 00:22:13.455
He doesn't have to die.

392
00:22:17.431 --> 00:22:18.750
It's not Wegener's.

393
00:22:33.959 --> 00:22:36.904
If it's Wegener's, his lungs won't be able to handle it.

394
00:22:37.396 --> 00:22:39.630
As soon as they pull that plug he'll die.

395
00:22:40.289 --> 00:22:42.833
That's why they call it pulling the plug.

396
00:23:04.134 --> 00:23:05.830
I'm gonna miss you.

397
00:23:43.974 --> 00:23:45.164
He's still breathing!

398
00:23:45.165 --> 00:23:46.322
His 0-2 stats are holding.

399
00:23:52.164 --> 00:23:53.162
He's holding his own.

400
00:23:53.521 --> 00:23:54.696
He's still breathing.

401
00:23:56.494 --> 00:23:57.806
It's not Wegener's.

402
00:24:05.917 --> 00:24:07.198
Wrong again.

403
00:24:10.473 --> 00:24:13.269
He's stable, but one of his arms is now paralyzed.

404
00:24:13.396 --> 00:24:16.525
The real question is, why is he still alive?

405
00:24:16.567 --> 00:24:18.333
Do you think he's just being stubborn?

406
00:24:18.874 --> 00:24:21.580
He's alive because you were wrong.
It's not Wegener's.

407
00:24:21.622 --> 00:24:25.933
Yeah, seem to be doing that a lot, lately.
People keep living because of my mistakes.

408
00:24:25.934 --> 00:24:28.333
Progression of the paralysis pretty much confirms Hamilton was right.

409
00:24:28.376 --> 00:24:29.315
It's ALS.

410
00:24:29.358 --> 00:24:31.616
Assuming this is a progression of his paralysis.

411
00:24:31.617 --> 00:24:32.886
He can't move his arm.

412
00:24:32.887 --> 00:24:36.226
Yes, his arm is paralyzed.
Yes, his legs are paralyzed.

413
00:24:36.269 --> 00:24:39.130
Why is everyone so gung-ho to connect those two conditions?

414
00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:42.474
You could think I'm wrong,
but that's no reason to stop thinking.

415
00:24:42.516 --> 00:24:44.916
How about this one?
He's not our patient.

416
00:24:44.959 --> 00:24:46.605
Nope, not good enough.

417
00:24:46.659 --> 00:24:48.845
He could have suffered a stroke when he was intubated.

418
00:24:49.482 --> 00:24:51.606
Well, blood clots are common in intubated patients.

419
00:24:51.607 --> 00:24:52.742
The inactivity causes...

420
00:24:52.785 --> 00:24:54.141
Not interested in why.

421
00:24:54.329 --> 00:24:56.867
Let's get an MR-angiogram for an embolic stroke.

422
00:24:56.910 --> 00:24:58.258
He doesn't want you treating him!

423
00:24:58.300 --> 00:24:59.384
They dropped the court order.

424
00:24:59.427 --> 00:25:01.561
Yeah, and that girl dropped the charges against Kobe.

425
00:25:01.562 --> 00:25:03.873
Doesn't mean that he should call her and see if she's free to get a sundae.

426
00:25:03.942 --> 00:25:06.711
Good point, but I can go within 50 feet of him now.

427
00:25:13.958 --> 00:25:15.907
If you need anything, page me. Any time.

428
00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:16.308
Okay.

429
00:25:34.305 --> 00:25:35.133
Get outta here.

430
00:25:36.117 --> 00:25:40.263
Sure.That makes sense.
You hate me for saving your life.

431
00:25:41.025 --> 00:25:43.997
In fairness to your side,
you were also dying because of me, so...

432
00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:45.484
You know I didn't wanna be saved.

433
00:25:45.527 --> 00:25:46.884
That's what's interesting.

434
00:25:47.302 --> 00:25:50.827
Your thyroid was low, but not low enough to cause depression.

435
00:25:51.056 --> 00:25:55.448
So, you came here to tell me that even if I can't walk I can still hear the birds sing?

436
00:25:55.941 --> 00:25:59.099
Enjoy the rainbow, and feeling the sun shine on my face?

437
00:26:00.336 --> 00:26:01.639
Those things are fun.

438
00:26:03.442 --> 00:26:07.906
Okay, life sucks.
Your life sucks more than most.

439
00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:12.391
It's not as bad as some,
which is depressing all by itself.

440
00:26:13.161 --> 00:26:14.629
But do me a favor.

441
00:26:16.318 --> 00:26:18.125
Just let me find out what's wrong with you.

442
00:26:19.595 --> 00:26:26.116
And if you still want to kill yourself,
I'll give you a hand.

443
00:26:27.239 --> 00:26:28.045
That sound fair?

444
00:26:28.045 --> 00:26:29.927
Yeah, sure.

445
00:26:30.461 --> 00:26:32.931
I'll stick around to indulge your obsession.

446
00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:36.550
It's over.
I lost my air.

447
00:26:37.591 --> 00:26:41.704
The session the other night, with those kids?
That was a test to see if I could still play.

448
00:26:42.388 --> 00:26:43.344
I can't.

449
00:26:43.845 --> 00:26:45.201
And that's all you are?

450
00:26:45.744 --> 00:26:46.623
A musician?

451
00:26:47.118 --> 00:26:50.445
I got one thing, same as you.

452
00:26:50.833 --> 00:26:51.381
Really?

453
00:26:53.355 --> 00:26:55.612
Apparently, you know me better than I know you.

454
00:26:55.612 --> 00:27:00.117
I know that limp.
I know the empty ring finger.

455
00:27:00.846 --> 00:27:03.622
And that obsessive nature of yours,
that's a big secret.

456
00:27:05.154 --> 00:27:13.415
You don't risk jail and your career just to save somebody who doesn't want to be saved unless you got something, anything, one thing.

457
00:27:14.225 --> 00:27:19.005
The reason normal people got wives and kids and hobbies, whatever.

458
00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:24.279
That's because they don't got that one thing
that hits them that hard and that true.

459
00:27:25.265 --> 00:27:27.367
I got music, you got this.

460
00:27:28.066 --> 00:27:32.025
The thing you think about all the time,
the thing that keeps you south of normal.

461
00:27:32.562 --> 00:27:38.447
Yeah, makes us great, makes us the best.
All we miss out on is everything else.

462
00:27:40.219 --> 00:27:43.495
No woman waiting at home
after work with the drink and the kiss,

463
00:27:44.028 --> 00:27:45.709
that ain't gonna happen for us.

464
00:27:47.231 --> 00:27:49.038
That's why God made microwaves.

465
00:27:49.926 --> 00:27:56.356
Yeah, but when it's over, it's over.

466
00:28:01.494 --> 00:28:02.524
Yeah.

467
00:28:20.229 --> 00:28:21.213
What are you doing?

468
00:28:23.504 --> 00:28:24.779
It's not over for me.

469
00:28:24.781 --> 00:28:28.401
Either you're gonna call the cops again, or we're doing this.

470
00:28:28.822 --> 00:28:32.031
If you wanna die, you can do it just as easily inside an MRI machine.

471
00:28:41.047 --> 00:28:44.787
So, I go back to L.A.
I'm going the Dana lectures again this year.

472
00:28:44.947 --> 00:28:48.248
Wow. That's quite an honor.

473
00:28:48.249 --> 00:28:49.597
It's all political.

474
00:28:49.597 --> 00:28:51.288
To be honest, you've earned it.

475
00:28:51.288 --> 00:28:53.481
I got honest on a couple studies I was doing.

476
00:28:53.933 --> 00:28:55.141
It all sells tickets.

477
00:28:55.626 --> 00:28:57.100
You remember last year's lectures, don't you?

478
00:28:57.100 --> 00:28:59.131
I remember the cigar bar afterwards.

479
00:28:59.131 --> 00:29:01.363
The only reason to attend the thing: cigars and pool.

480
00:29:05.149 --> 00:29:06.653
You enjoy working with Dr. House?

481
00:29:09.205 --> 00:29:10.743
Not the word I'd use.

482
00:29:11.428 --> 00:29:13.521
We work too hard not to enjoy ourselves, right?

483
00:29:15.220 --> 00:29:17.308
Hmm. You seeing anybody?

484
00:29:17.308 --> 00:29:18.456
Kinda sorta.

485
00:29:18.457 --> 00:29:19.374
Serious?

486
00:29:20.852 --> 00:29:22.044
I don't know. Could be.

487
00:29:22.044 --> 00:29:23.965
You think she would enjoy L.A.?

488
00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:44.160
Hamilton offered me a job.

489
00:29:45.160 --> 00:29:45.864
You gonna take it?

490
00:29:45.864 --> 00:29:47.091
I haven't decided.

491
00:29:47.092 --> 00:29:48.979
You already have the most prestigious fellowship.

492
00:29:48.979 --> 00:29:50.206
It's a partnership.

493
00:29:50.592 --> 00:29:54.631
Three times the money, car allowance,
moving expense, pension plan,

494
00:29:54.673 --> 00:29:57.376
the chance to work for a guy who gives a crap what other people think.

495
00:29:57.401 --> 00:29:59.407
So, why didn't you just tell him yes?

496
00:29:59.902 --> 00:30:01.322
I made a commitment here.

497
00:30:02.199 --> 00:30:03.027
Right.

498
00:30:03.027 --> 00:30:04.757
House would let you out of it in a heartbeat.

499
00:30:04.757 --> 00:30:07.161
Or he wouldn't, just to jerk me around.

500
00:30:07.683 --> 00:30:09.180
Would you guys have taken the job?

501
00:30:09.365 --> 00:30:10.180
Don't need the money.

502
00:30:10.223 --> 00:30:12.348
I'm not like you.
I don't hate House.

503
00:30:13.239 --> 00:30:15.666
You guys really don't have a problem with him, with what he did?

504
00:30:16.221 --> 00:30:18.046
He knows we disagree with his choice.

505
00:30:18.046 --> 00:30:18.980
Choice?

506
00:30:19.058 --> 00:30:21.522
Chase, it's not apples and oranges, its right and wrong.

507
00:30:21.650 --> 00:30:23.041
And he does it like he doesn't care!

508
00:30:23.445 --> 00:30:26.214
He assaults the guy and moves on to the next differential diagnosis like it's nothing...

509
00:30:26.214 --> 00:30:27.487
What do you want from him?

510
00:30:27.615 --> 00:30:30.777
More hand-wringing, more torment?
You want him to cry himself to sleep at night?

511
00:30:30.820 --> 00:30:34.328
Yeah! Yeah, I want some clue that he knows it's a big deal.

512
00:30:34.329 --> 00:30:36.596
That it scares him, that it matters.

513
00:30:36.597 --> 00:30:39.212
Whoa, whoa, whoa, what's that?

514
00:30:41.136 --> 00:30:42.358
It was a stroke!

515
00:30:43.810 --> 00:30:44.961
Well done, Cameron.

516
00:30:44.961 --> 00:30:47.773
House called it.
The arm and legs are unrelated.

517
00:30:48.733 --> 00:30:51.094
You make enough calls,
one of them is bound to be right.

518
00:30:51.094 --> 00:30:54.249
Yeah, he's just a lucky, lucky guy.

519
00:30:54.250 --> 00:30:58.616
Listen, listen, I just think it wouldn't hurt him to learn a little humility.

520
00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:01.227
So, what's the verdict?

521
00:31:02.490 --> 00:31:05.438
Embolus.
The arm problem's caused by a clot.

522
00:31:05.608 --> 00:31:08.205
There's a good chance we can still bust the clot with TPA.

523
00:31:08.936 --> 00:31:11.288
Do it. See what happens to his arm.

524
00:31:21.563 --> 00:31:23.509
We found a blood clot in your brain.

525
00:31:24.009 --> 00:31:26.560
We'd like to start you on heparin,
it's a blood thinner.

526
00:31:27.588 --> 00:31:28.905
What are the side effects?

527
00:31:29.478 --> 00:31:32.088
Well, your lungs are kind of chewed up from the pneumonia.

528
00:31:32.343 --> 00:31:33.841
Good chance there'll be an infusion.

529
00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:34.941
Bleeding?

530
00:31:35.326 --> 00:31:36.561
Into his lungs.

531
00:31:36.562 --> 00:31:39.261
It's our best chance to give you back the use of your arm.

532
00:31:39.261 --> 00:31:41.295
I don't need my arm without my air.

533
00:31:41.379 --> 00:31:42.937
The damage would likely be minimal.

534
00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:44.044
No.

535
00:31:46.991 --> 00:31:47.832
It's his call.

536
00:31:50.633 --> 00:31:52.522
Or we could do an embolectomy.

537
00:31:52.784 --> 00:31:55.363
Basically, we'd go in and pull the clot out.

538
00:31:55.781 --> 00:31:58.902
He's saying no to drugs and you're recommending brain surgery?

539
00:31:59.244 --> 00:32:01.256
Bypasses risk to the lungs.

540
00:32:01.341 --> 00:32:02.453
It'll either help your arm, or...

541
00:32:02.496 --> 00:32:03.565
Or it'll kill me.

542
00:32:03.989 --> 00:32:06.468
From your point of view,
it's a win-win.

543
00:32:08.551 --> 00:32:09.747
Okay.

544
00:32:10.475 --> 00:32:12.128
We'll set up the procedure for today.

545
00:32:24.271 --> 00:32:26.035
We're in your carotid artery.

546
00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:29.379
This is going to take us all the way up into your brain.

547
00:32:30.751 --> 00:32:31.686
You all right?

548
00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:34.075
Yes.

549
00:32:41.441 --> 00:32:45.216
The sludge is blocking the blood flow to the part of the brain that controls your arm.

550
00:32:55.005 --> 00:32:56.032
All done.

551
00:32:56.753 --> 00:32:58.126
You still okay?

552
00:33:00.122 --> 00:33:01.483
I think so.

553
00:33:02.433 --> 00:33:03.570
I don't know, I guess...

554
00:33:04.262 --> 00:33:07.652
It's just with Dr. Hamilton here,
I notice a difference in styles.

555
00:33:08.950 --> 00:33:11.524
His style involves humility.

556
00:33:12.733 --> 00:33:16.771
Another difference in our styles:
I don't care much for apologies.

557
00:33:17.233 --> 00:33:17.895
You can go.

558
00:33:18.023 --> 00:33:20.465
I didn't know you were standing there when I said that, okay?

559
00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:21.782
It was completely rude.

560
00:33:21.867 --> 00:33:23.687
Do you mean genuine humility?

561
00:33:23.689 --> 00:33:26.292
You know, with all the self-doubt and the self-questioning,

562
00:33:26.335 --> 00:33:30.178
or Hamilton's polite, patronizing "Aw, shucks" humility?

563
00:33:30.263 --> 00:33:31.490
You're both excellent doctors.

564
00:33:31.619 --> 00:33:32.283
Thank you.

565
00:33:32.502 --> 00:33:36.785
And humility is an important quality.
Especially if you're wrong a lot.

566
00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:38.786
You've been wrong every step of the way.

567
00:33:44.122 --> 00:33:49.059
Of course, when you're right, self-doubt doesn't help anybody, does it?

568
00:33:55.613 --> 00:33:56.651
We gotta go.

569
00:34:04.569 --> 00:34:05.796
Congratulations, Gregg.

570
00:34:07.637 --> 00:34:09.045
Congratulations...

571
00:34:09.046 --> 00:34:10.160
Why, Marty?

572
00:34:10.160 --> 00:34:12.558
John woke up, he can use his arm.

573
00:34:13.684 --> 00:34:14.833
Thank you.

574
00:34:14.834 --> 00:34:16.804
Now look, I know we've had our differences,

575
00:34:16.806 --> 00:34:20.160
but I think when a person does something well it ought to be recognized.

576
00:34:21.084 --> 00:34:22.308
The phone works.

577
00:34:22.589 --> 00:34:25.980
Next time you want to make me feel all warm and fuzzy, leave a message.

578
00:34:26.272 --> 00:34:28.845
Don't get me wrong,
I'm overcome with joy.

579
00:34:29.088 --> 00:34:29.891
Doc?

580
00:34:30.076 --> 00:34:30.424
Yeah?

581
00:34:30.424 --> 00:34:31.114
What?

582
00:34:32.192 --> 00:34:33.688
You just touched my leg.

583
00:34:34.642 --> 00:34:36.645
What, you gonna charge me with assault again?

584
00:34:37.294 --> 00:34:40.840
No, I felt it.

585
00:34:48.649 --> 00:34:50.919
He now has feeling all the way up to calf.

586
00:34:50.921 --> 00:34:53.614
This is the way medicine evolved.

587
00:34:54.036 --> 00:34:55.855
Patients sometimes get better.

588
00:34:55.899 --> 00:34:59.037
You have no idea why, but unless you give a reason they won't pay you.

589
00:34:59.215 --> 00:35:01.268
Anybody notice if there's a full moon?

590
00:35:01.481 --> 00:35:03.459
You're saying he just spontaneously got better?

591
00:35:03.502 --> 00:35:06.325
No, I'm saying let's rule out the lunar god and go from there.

592
00:35:06.325 --> 00:35:07.932
Something he's on is working.

593
00:35:08.463 --> 00:35:11.536
The enzyme replacement protocol is working, reversing the ALS.

594
00:35:11.579 --> 00:35:13.314
The timing doesn't suspicious to you?

595
00:35:13.356 --> 00:35:15.397
Heh heh, do you think Dr. House will see it that way?

596
00:35:15.686 --> 00:35:17.378
Figure it's his medication doing the job?

597
00:35:17.738 --> 00:35:18.961
I'm sure he will.

598
00:35:19.088 --> 00:35:20.444
It's one of ours.

599
00:35:20.570 --> 00:35:22.696
How do we figure out which drug is doing the trick?

600
00:35:22.739 --> 00:35:24.989
Easy. We stop all of them.

601
00:35:25.032 --> 00:35:26.348
One of those drugs is helping him.

602
00:35:26.391 --> 00:35:27.443
And the rest?

603
00:35:27.862 --> 00:35:31.946
Steroids, antibiotics, anti-inflammatories,
they're toxic if we leave him on all of them.

604
00:35:31.988 --> 00:35:32.838
He'll walk again.

605
00:35:32.881 --> 00:35:34.120
Yeah, to his own funeral.

606
00:35:34.163 --> 00:35:35.862
But if we stop everything, he'll get worse.

607
00:35:35.904 --> 00:35:38.524
True. And then we'll add our medications back, one at a time.

608
00:35:38.568 --> 00:35:40.381
If he gets better, then we'll have our answer.

609
00:35:40.423 --> 00:35:41.658
And if he doesn't get better?

610
00:35:42.235 --> 00:35:43.354
Then we're in trouble.

611
00:35:44.038 --> 00:35:45.645
Not as much as he is.

612
00:35:46.636 --> 00:35:48.033
I suppose that was your point.

613
00:35:51.242 --> 00:35:54.896
I assume you want me to convince Hamilton to stop his treatments, too?

614
00:35:55.085 --> 00:35:55.775
Don't care.

615
00:35:55.859 --> 00:35:58.667
You know at least five of the medications are part of his protocol?

616
00:35:58.780 --> 00:36:01.613
If his stuff was helping the guy would be playing tennis six months ago.

617
00:36:01.829 --> 00:36:03.945
It's our round of treatment that's helping.

618
00:36:03.945 --> 00:36:06.305
Right. So that's what you'll stop.

619
00:36:09.693 --> 00:36:11.045
So your philosophy is,

620
00:36:11.046 --> 00:36:13.432
"If they don't want treatment,
they get it shoved down their throat,

621
00:36:13.474 --> 00:36:16.250
but if it might cure their paralysis,
whoa, better slow down."

622
00:36:16.679 --> 00:36:17.290
Yeah.

623
00:36:17.879 --> 00:36:20.612
My old philosophy used to be "Live and let live,"

624
00:36:21.109 --> 00:36:24.965
but I'm taking this needlepoint class and they gave us these really big pillows.

625
00:36:25.311 --> 00:36:27.240
What's your philosophy on employee relations?

626
00:36:27.283 --> 00:36:28.757
That's a very tiny pillow.

627
00:36:29.293 --> 00:36:31.720
Great. Sounds like you know what you're doing.

628
00:36:32.842 --> 00:36:34.988
Gregg. Can I have a word?

629
00:36:35.107 --> 00:36:37.409
Can it wait for the commercial, Marty?

630
00:36:38.105 --> 00:36:39.647
Say "hi" to my friend, Jimmy.

631
00:36:41.420 --> 00:36:42.341
Pleasure.

632
00:36:43.321 --> 00:36:45.673
Your diagnostic trial...

633
00:36:45.673 --> 00:36:47.978
Seriously, commercial's in about five minutes.

634
00:36:48.021 --> 00:36:48.987
...it's a dangerous game.

635
00:36:49.030 --> 00:36:50.834
Only if we're watching in the bathtub.

636
00:36:51.635 --> 00:36:54.530
I need to know exactly what medications you were giving John Henry.

637
00:36:54.531 --> 00:36:55.605
Forget it.

638
00:36:55.606 --> 00:36:58.266
And if Foreman tells you what John Henry used to be on...

639
00:36:58.267 --> 00:36:59.378
You're gonna fire him?

640
00:37:00.167 --> 00:37:03.719
Nope. Make him live out every day of his two-year contract.

641
00:37:03.719 --> 00:37:04.788
Foreman asked to leave?

642
00:37:04.873 --> 00:37:05.615
Not yet.

643
00:37:05.655 --> 00:37:06.659
But how do you know I made him an...

644
00:37:06.702 --> 00:37:09.410
You're a polite twit,
but you're not an idiot.

645
00:37:10.168 --> 00:37:13.056
I need to know what medications you've taken him off of.

646
00:37:16.314 --> 00:37:18.815
Wait a second.
You're not an idiot.

647
00:37:22.431 --> 00:37:26.846
You need to know what medications I've stopped, because...

648
00:37:29.656 --> 00:37:30.840
He's getting worse.

649
00:37:36.536 --> 00:37:38.261
Right now you still work for me.
Come on!

650
00:37:41.390 --> 00:37:43.229
No feeling, no wiggling.

651
00:37:43.272 --> 00:37:45.425
The bad news is, John Henry's back where he started.

652
00:37:45.469 --> 00:37:47.096
The good news is, Hamilton looks bad.

653
00:37:47.443 --> 00:37:50.109
Yeah, it's not whether you win or lose,
it's whether the other guy loses.

654
00:37:50.152 --> 00:37:51.422
What was the first thing we put him on?

655
00:37:51.465 --> 00:37:52.884
Steroids, for the pneumonia.

656
00:37:52.926 --> 00:37:55.610
Well, put him back on them.
Give them 24 hours, see what happens.

657
00:37:55.611 --> 00:37:56.834
His lungs aren't great.

658
00:37:57.214 --> 00:37:58.644
It might cause another respiratory collapse.

659
00:37:58.686 --> 00:37:59.573
Sure hope not.

660
00:37:59.617 --> 00:38:01.737
I didn't much like that lawyer. And get an MRI.

661
00:38:01.863 --> 00:38:03.369
He already got one. And an MRA.

662
00:38:03.412 --> 00:38:05.578
Well, obviously something is different now. Do it again.

663
00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:08.355
Chase, take care of the scan.
Cameron, put him on the steroids.

664
00:38:12.185 --> 00:38:13.684
We should probably talk, huh?

665
00:38:19.755 --> 00:38:20.868
Thin cuts.

666
00:38:21.256 --> 00:38:22.099
What levels?

667
00:38:22.100 --> 00:38:25.257
T9 through the quarter-equinus, same as the last time.

668
00:38:26.930 --> 00:38:29.479
You really think House would let Foreman out of his contract?

669
00:38:29.648 --> 00:38:30.533
He has to.

670
00:38:30.535 --> 00:38:32.605
If he doesn't, he's telling Foreman he needs him.

671
00:38:33.102 --> 00:38:34.378
House can't handle that.

672
00:38:40.548 --> 00:38:41.705
Oh, my God!

673
00:38:47.497 --> 00:38:48.727
I checked him out.

674
00:38:50.599 --> 00:38:52.208
He's a great doctor.

675
00:38:53.886 --> 00:38:55.396
You think he's better than I am.

676
00:38:56.356 --> 00:38:57.629
This about your ego?

677
00:38:58.543 --> 00:38:59.851
Answer the question.

678
00:39:01.781 --> 00:39:03.983
It's not going to change my opinion of myself.

679
00:39:04.328 --> 00:39:08.371
Might affect my opinion of you, but that shouldn't affect your opinion of yourself.

680
00:39:09.933 --> 00:39:11.283
Now I'm getting confused.

681
00:39:11.423 --> 00:39:14.536
If you think he's a better doctor than I am, then you should take the job.

682
00:39:14.918 --> 00:39:20.482
Otherwise, you should get him to buy you two or three more nostalgic lunches and politely decline.

683
00:39:20.573 --> 00:39:21.322
It's that simple?

684
00:39:21.407 --> 00:39:23.869
I should just ignore the mockery and abuse?

685
00:39:23.912 --> 00:39:25.506
Oh, how do I abuse you?

686
00:39:25.549 --> 00:39:27.783
How do you not?
If I make a mistake...

687
00:39:27.825 --> 00:39:29.720
I hold you accountable, so what?

688
00:39:29.763 --> 00:39:33.067
Dr. Hamilton forgives.
He's capable of moving on.

689
00:39:33.068 --> 00:39:34.457
That is not what he does!

690
00:39:34.499 --> 00:39:35.893
I screwed up his case, he told me...

691
00:39:35.935 --> 00:39:39.636
He never said you were forgiven.
I was there, he said it wasn't your fault.

692
00:39:39.679 --> 00:39:40.028
So?

693
00:39:40.070 --> 00:39:41.786
So, it was.

694
00:39:42.734 --> 00:39:44.747
You took a chance,
you did something great.

695
00:39:44.749 --> 00:39:46.903
You were wrong,
but it was still great.

696
00:39:46.904 --> 00:39:50.002
You should feel great that it was great.
You should feel like crap that it was wrong.

697
00:39:50.741 --> 00:39:52.511
That's the difference between him and me.

698
00:39:52.595 --> 00:39:55.136
He thinks you do your job,
and what will be, will be.

699
00:39:55.138 --> 00:39:57.404
I think that what I do and what you do matters.

700
00:39:59.509 --> 00:40:02.287
He sleeps better at night.
He shouldn't.

701
00:40:05.322 --> 00:40:06.834
Arteriovenous malformation.

702
00:40:06.834 --> 00:40:09.009
Intradural, compressing his spine.

703
00:40:11.181 --> 00:40:12.757
Causing his paralysis.

704
00:40:13.488 --> 00:40:15.760
How could Hamilton have missed an AVM?

705
00:40:15.947 --> 00:40:19.795
Well, we missed it, too.
'Cause it wasn't there before.

706
00:40:20.505 --> 00:40:22.274
You saying it just grew overnight?

707
00:40:22.747 --> 00:40:27.525
No. It was on the spine,
it wasn't on the MRI.

708
00:40:28.402 --> 00:40:30.347
Same anatomic location.

709
00:40:31.027 --> 00:40:32.028
It's not there.

710
00:40:32.817 --> 00:40:33.999
Well, what is there?

711
00:40:33.999 --> 00:40:34.605
Nothing.

712
00:40:35.942 --> 00:40:38.832
Or something that looks like nothing.

713
00:40:40.497 --> 00:40:41.749
Background noise, static.

714
00:40:41.791 --> 00:40:43.879
Scar tissue? Inflammation?

715
00:40:43.922 --> 00:40:46.396
If it's inflammation, the steroids would have shrunk it down.

716
00:40:47.893 --> 00:40:49.397
Revealing the AVM,

717
00:40:50.171 --> 00:40:54.438
which has always been there,
hiding behind its own swelling.
We remove that, he'll walk again.

718
00:41:44.163 --> 00:41:45.545
Dr. House!

719
00:41:47.651 --> 00:41:49.737
Cora's meeting me outside with a limo.

720
00:41:50.948 --> 00:41:52.376
I'm being discharged.

721
00:41:53.695 --> 00:41:55.722
Fifty bucks says I can beat you to the curb.

722
00:41:57.694 --> 00:41:59.339
Thanks for sticking with the case.

723
00:41:59.886 --> 00:42:01.150
I can't do anything else.

724
00:42:04.001 --> 00:42:06.108
You're much more easily amused when you can walk.

725
00:42:06.277 --> 00:42:07.485
How bizarre.

726
00:42:08.559 --> 00:42:12.723
I'm guessing you weren't exactly Mr. Sunshine even before your leg got messed up.

727
00:42:17.527 --> 00:42:18.834
I want you to have this.

728
00:42:29.459 --> 00:42:30.280
Wow.

729
00:42:30.484 --> 00:42:33.865
You can sell it if you want to,
just promise me you won't play it.

730
00:42:39.128 --> 00:42:40.784
How many of those pills you taking?

731
00:42:43.231 --> 00:42:44.307
I'm in pain.

732
00:42:46.702 --> 00:42:47.520
Yeah.

733
00:42:50.053 --> 00:42:51.242
Aren't we all?

734
00:42:53.739 --> 00:42:56.705
So, where do you buy these things,
do they have cane stores?

735
00:42:56.749 --> 00:42:59.720
Oh, don't worry about it. You'll be jogging before you need a second one.

736
00:43:00.900 --> 00:43:02.677
See you tomorrow, Eric.

9999
00:00:0.500 --> 00:00:2.00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>

